<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>grammar</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>grammar</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Active and Passive Voice</li><li>Conjunctions</li><li>Determiners</li><li>Modal Verbs</li><li>Parts of Speech</li><li>Prepositions</li><li>Subject-Verb Agreement</li><li>Tenses</li></ul><h2>Active and Passive Voice</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Voice?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Voice in grammar refers to the relationship between the subject of a sentence and the action of the verb. Active voice means the subject performs the action (e.g., &#x27;The cat chased the mouse&#x27;). Passive voice means the subject receives the action (e.g., &#x27;The mouse was chased by the cat&#x27;). Voice is a fundamental concept tested in NEB English exams through transformation exercises, where students must convert active sentences to passive or vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Structure of Passive Voice</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The passive voice is formed using: be (in the appropriate tense) + past participle of the main verb. The object of the active sentence becomes the subject of the passive sentence. The original subject (the doer) becomes the agent, introduced by &#x27;by&#x27; , and can be omitted when it is unknown, unimportant, or obvious. Example: Active — &#x27;Rama wrote the letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;The letter was written by Rama.&#x27; Key rule: the tense of &#x27;be&#x27; must match the tense of the active verb.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Passive Voice in Different Tenses</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Present Simple: Active — &#x27;She writes a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter is written by her.&#x27; Present Continuous: Active — &#x27;She is writing a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter is being written by her.&#x27; Present Perfect: Active — &#x27;She has written a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter has been written by her.&#x27; Past Simple: Active — &#x27;She wrote a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter was written by her.&#x27; Past Continuous: Active — &#x27;She was writing a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter was being written by her.&#x27; Past Perfect: Active — &#x27;She had written a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter had been written by her.&#x27; Future Simple: Active — &#x27;She will write a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter will be written by her.&#x27; Future Perfect: Active — &#x27;She will have written a letter.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;A letter will have been written by her.&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Passive with Modals</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Modal verbs in passive voice follow the pattern: modal + be + past participle . Examples: &#x27;You must finish the work&#x27; → &#x27;The work must be finished (by you)&#x27;. &#x27;She can swim&#x27; → &#x27;Swimming can be done by her&#x27; (though this sounds awkward — passives with ability modals are often avoided). &#x27;They should help the poor&#x27; → &#x27;The poor should be helped (by them)&#x27;. &#x27;He may come late&#x27; → &#x27;Late arrival may be expected from him&#x27;. This structure is frequently tested in NEB transformation exercises.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Passive with Infinitives and Gerunds</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>With infinitives: Active — &#x27;I want to see him.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;He is wanted to be seen by me&#x27; (rare and awkward, often rephrased). Better: Active — &#x27;They expect him to come.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;He is expected to come.&#x27; With gerunds: Active — &#x27;I suggest visiting the temple.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;Visiting the temple is suggested.&#x27; Active — &#x27;He denied stealing the money.&#x27; → Passive — &#x27;Stealing the money was denied by him.&#x27; Note: Passive with gerunds is uncommon in natural English but may appear in academic contexts.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Intransitive Verbs and Passive Voice</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Intransitive verbs (verbs that do not take an object) CANNOT be converted to passive voice because passive requires an object to become a subject. Examples of intransitive verbs: arrive, sleep, die, go, come, happen, occur, fall, rise, exist. &#x27;The bird flew&#x27; cannot become &#x27;___ was flown by the bird.&#x27; However, some verbs that are usually intransitive can be used transitively in specific contexts: &#x27;The sun rises&#x27; (intransitive) vs. &#x27;She rises the children&#x27; (rare/archaic transitive use). Also, intransitive verbs taking a preposition can sometimes be made passive: &#x27;They laughed at him&#x27; → &#x27;He was laughed at by them&#x27;.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Conjunctions</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Conjunctions?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Conjunctions are words that connect words, phrases, or clauses. They are essential for creating complex and compound sentences, and their correct use is fundamental to clear, coherent writing. Conjunctions are categorised into three main types: coordinating , subordinating , and correlative . Mastery of conjunctions is essential for NEB English exams, where sentence combination and transformation questions are common.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Coordinating Conjunctions (FANBOYS)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Coordinating conjunctions join elements of equal grammatical status — words, phrases, or independent clauses. The seven coordinating conjunctions are remembered by the acronym FANBOYS : For (reason/purpose), And (addition), Nor (negative addition), But (contrast), Or (alternative), Yet (contrast/surprise), So (result). When joining two independent clauses, a comma precedes the conjunction: &#x27;I wanted to go, but I was too tired.&#x27; When joining only two verbs or two objects, no comma is needed: &#x27;She sang and danced.&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Subordinating Conjunctions</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Subordinating conjunctions introduce dependent (subordinate) clauses and connect them to independent (main) clauses. They express relationships of time, cause, condition, contrast, and purpose. Time : when, while, as, before, after, until, since, once, whenever. Cause/Reason : because, since, as, so that, in order that. Condition : if, unless, provided that, as long as, only if. Contrast : although, though, even though, whereas, while. Purpose : so that, in order that, lest. Example: &#x27; Although it was raining, we went out&#x27; (contrast). &#x27;I will wait until you return&#x27; (time).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Punctuation with Subordinating Conjunctions</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>The placement of the subordinate clause determines punctuation. When the subordinate clause comes before the main clause, use a comma: &#x27; Because he studied hard, he passed the exam.&#x27; When the subordinate clause comes after the main clause, no comma is needed: &#x27;He passed the exam because he studied hard.&#x27; This rule applies to all subordinating conjunctions. However, if the subordinate clause adds non-essential information, it may be set off with commas even when it follows the main clause.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Correlative Conjunctions</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Correlative conjunctions are pairs of conjunctions that work together to join equal grammatical elements. Common pairs: either...or (alternative), neither...nor (negative alternative), both...and (addition), not only...but also (addition with emphasis), whether...or (alternative), as...as (comparison), so...that (result), such...that (result). Key rule: correlative conjunctions require parallel structure . &#x27;Not only did he study , but he also passed with distinction.&#x27; Both parts must be grammatically parallel.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Parallel Structure with Correlative Conjunctions</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Parallel structure (parallelism) is the principle that elements joined by correlative conjunctions must have the same grammatical form. Examples: Correct — &#x27;She is not only intelligent but also hardworking .&#x27; (both adjectives). Wrong — &#x27;She is not only intelligent but also hardworking person.&#x27; (mismatch). Correct — &#x27; Either you come with us or you stay here.&#x27; (both verbs). Wrong — &#x27;Either you come with us or you staying here.&#x27; Parallelism is essential for grammatical correctness and clarity in writing.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Determiners</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Determiners?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Determiners are words placed before nouns to specify or quantify them. They answer questions like &#x27;Which one?&#x27;, &#x27;How many?&#x27;, or &#x27;Whose?&#x27; Determiners include articles (a, an, the), demonstratives (this, that, these, those), possessives (my, your, his, her, its, our, their), quantifiers (some, any, many, much, few, little, several, all, both, each, every, either, neither), and numbers (one, two, first, second). Determiners occupy the same position in the noun phrase and generally cannot be used together — only one determiner can precede a noun.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Articles: A, An, The</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Indefinite articles (&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;an&#x27;) are used before singular, countable nouns when referring to something non-specific or mentioned for the first time. Use &#x27;a&#x27; before consonant sounds (&#x27;a university&#x27;, &#x27;a one-way street&#x27;). Use &#x27;an&#x27; before vowel sounds (&#x27;an hour&#x27;, &#x27;an MBA&#x27;). Definite article (&#x27;the&#x27;) is used for specific or previously mentioned nouns, unique things (&#x27;the sun&#x27;), superlatives (&#x27;the best&#x27;), and ordinal numbers (&#x27;the first&#x27;). Zero article (no article) is used before generic plurals (&#x27;Dogs are loyal&#x27;), abstract nouns in general sense (&#x27;Honesty is good&#x27;), names of meals (&#x27;have breakfast&#x27;), most proper nouns (&#x27;Kathmandu is beautiful&#x27;), and languages (&#x27;I study English&#x27;).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Some vs. Any: Quantifiers</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Some is used in affirmative sentences and offers/requests: &#x27;I have some books.&#x27; &#x27;Would you like some tea?&#x27; Any is used in negative sentences and questions: &#x27;I don&#x27;t have any money.&#x27; &#x27;Do you have any ideas?&#x27; However, &#x27;some&#x27; is used in questions when offering or requesting: &#x27;Would you like some water?&#x27; (offering). &#x27;Can I have some help?&#x27; (request). &#x27;Any&#x27; is also used in affirmative sentences with the meaning &#x27;it doesn&#x27;t matter which&#x27;: &#x27;Choose any book you like.&#x27; &#x27;You can come any day.&#x27; This latter use is frequently tested.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Many vs. Much: Countable vs. Uncountable</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Many is used with countable plural nouns: &#x27;many books&#x27;, &#x27;many students&#x27;, &#x27;many ideas&#x27;. Much is used with uncountable nouns: &#x27;much time&#x27;, &#x27;much water&#x27;, &#x27;much information&#x27;. In affirmative sentences, &#x27;much&#x27; is less common than &#x27;a lot of&#x27; — &#x27;I have much time&#x27; sounds formal; &#x27;I have a lot of time&#x27; is more natural. &#x27;Many&#x27; and &#x27;much&#x27; are more common in questions and negative sentences: &#x27;How many students are there?&#x27; &#x27;I don&#x27;t have much time.&#x27; NEB exams frequently test the distinction through gap-filling exercises.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Few vs. A Few: Negative vs. Positive</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Few (without article) means &#x27;not many&#x27; and has a negative connotation: &#x27;I have few friends&#x27; = I almost have no friends (disappointment). A few means &#x27;some&#x27; and has a positive connotation: &#x27;I have a few friends&#x27; = I have some friends (satisfaction). The same distinction applies to little vs. a little with uncountable nouns: &#x27;I have little hope&#x27; (negative) vs. &#x27;I have a little hope&#x27; (positive). This distinction is one of the most commonly tested topics in NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Little vs. A Little: Uncountable Equivalents</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Little (without article) means &#x27;not much&#x27; (negative): &#x27;I have little time&#x27; = I don&#x27;t have enough time. A little means &#x27;some&#x27; (positive): &#x27;I have a little time&#x27; = I have some time available. Fewer is used with countable nouns: &#x27;fewer books&#x27;, &#x27;fewer students&#x27;. Less is used with uncountable nouns: &#x27;less water&#x27;, &#x27;less time&#x27;. However, &#x27;less&#x27; is used with expressions of time, money, and distance as single quantities: &#x27;less than 5 minutes&#x27;, &#x27;less than $10&#x27;. The distinction between &#x27;fewer&#x27; and &#x27;less&#x27; is frequently tested in NEB grammar exercises.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Few vs. A few: &#x27;Few&#x27; is negative (almost none): &#x27;I have few friends&#x27; = I am lonely. &#x27;A few&#x27; is positive (some): &#x27;I have a few friends&#x27; = I am not lonely. The article &#x27;a&#x27; changes the meaning entirely. This distinction is tested in every NEB exam.</td></tr><tr><td>Little vs. A little: &#x27;Little&#x27; is negative (not enough): &#x27;I have little money&#x27; = I am poor. &#x27;A little&#x27; is positive (some): &#x27;I have a little money&#x27; = I am not broke. Same pattern as few/a few, but for uncountable nouns.</td></tr><tr><td>Much vs. Many: &#x27;Much&#x27; is for uncountable nouns (much water, much time). &#x27;Many&#x27; is for countable nouns (many books, many students). In affirmative sentences, &#x27;a lot of&#x27; is often preferred over &#x27;much&#x27; for a more natural sound: &#x27;I have a lot of time&#x27; sounds more natural than &#x27;I have much time&#x27;.</td></tr><tr><td>Other vs. Another vs. The other vs. The others: &#x27;Other&#x27; (general additional): &#x27;I need other help.&#x27; &#x27;Another&#x27; (one more, singular): &#x27;Give me another chance.&#x27; &#x27;The other&#x27; (second of two, specific): &#x27;One shoe is missing; the other is here.&#x27; &#x27;The others&#x27; (remaining specific, plural): &#x27;Three students passed; the others failed.&#x27; Each has a specific meaning and grammatical function.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Modal Verbs</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Modal Verbs?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Modal verbs (or modal auxiliaries) are a special class of helping verbs that express necessity, possibility, permission, ability, advice, or obligation. The primary modals are: can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must . Semi-modals include ought to, need, have to, used to . Modals are unique because they do not change form for person or number (no -s in third person singular), they are followed by the base form of the main verb (without &#x27;to&#x27;, except &#x27;ought to&#x27;), and they do not take -ing or -ed endings.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Can and Could: Ability and Permission</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Can expresses ability in the present (&#x27;I can swim&#x27;) and informal permission (&#x27;Can I borrow your pen?&#x27;). Could is the past form of &#x27;can&#x27; for ability (&#x27;I could run fast when I was young&#x27;). For polite requests , &#x27;could&#x27; is softer and more polite than &#x27;can&#x27; (&#x27;Could you help me?&#x27;). &#x27;Could&#x27; also expresses possibility in the present (&#x27;It could rain tomorrow&#x27;). Note: &#x27;Could&#x27; for permission is more tentative than &#x27;can&#x27; — &#x27;Could I leave?&#x27; suggests the speaker is asking for permission with more hesitation.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>May and Might: Permission and Possibility</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>May expresses formal permission (&#x27;You may leave now&#x27;) and possibility (&#x27;It may rain today&#x27;). Might expresses remote possibility (&#x27;It might rain, but I doubt it&#x27;) and polite suggestions (&#x27;You might want to try this&#x27;). &#x27;Might&#x27; is less certain than &#x27;may&#x27;. In reported speech, &#x27;may&#x27; becomes &#x27;might&#x27;: &#x27;You may go&#x27; → He said I might go. For formal permission, &#x27;may&#x27; is preferred in NEB exams: &#x27;May I come in?&#x27; (not &#x27;Might I come in?&#x27; — though grammatically possible, it is archaic). &#x27;Might&#x27; is rarely used for permission in modern English.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Shall and Should: Suggestions and Obligation</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Shall is used with &#x27;I&#x27; and &#x27;we&#x27; for future intention in formal English (&#x27;I shall return&#x27;) and for suggestions and offers in questions (&#x27;Shall we go?&#x27; = &#x27;Do you suggest that we go?&#x27;). In modern English, especially American English, &#x27;shall&#x27; is increasingly replaced by &#x27;will&#x27;. Should is the past form of &#x27;shall&#x27; and is primarily used for advice (&#x27;You should study harder&#x27;), obligation (&#x27;You should apologise&#x27;), and expectation (&#x27;He should arrive by now&#x27;). &#x27;Should&#x27; is one of the most frequently tested modals in NEB exams, especially in gap-filling and error correction.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Will and Would: Future and Politeness</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Will expresses future actions (&#x27;I will visit Kathmandu next week&#x27;), willingness (&#x27;I will help you&#x27;), promises (&#x27;I will never forget you&#x27;), and predictions (&#x27;It will rain tomorrow&#x27;). Would is the past form of &#x27;will&#x27; and is used for conditional statements (&#x27;If I had money, I would travel&#x27;), polite requests (&#x27;Would you open the door?&#x27;), past habits (&#x27;He would always help others&#x27;), and reported speech (&#x27;She said she would come&#x27;). &#x27;Would&#x27; is essential for the second and third conditionals. In NEB exams, distinguishing &#x27;will&#x27; from &#x27;would&#x27; in context is a common task.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Must and Have To: Obligation</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Must expresses strong obligation from the speaker&#x27;s perspective (&#x27;You must stop at a red light&#x27;) and logical deduction (&#x27;He must be at home — his car is outside&#x27;). Have to expresses external obligation imposed by rules or circumstances (&#x27;I have to wear a uniform at school&#x27;). The key difference: &#x27;must&#x27; is subjective (speaker&#x27;s authority); &#x27;have to&#x27; is objective (external rule). &#x27;Must&#x27; has no past form — use &#x27;had to&#x27; for past obligation (&#x27;I had to finish the work yesterday&#x27;). For negative: &#x27;must not&#x27; = prohibition (&#x27;You must not smoke here&#x27;); &#x27;don&#x27;t have to&#x27; = absence of obligation (&#x27;You don&#x27;t have to come&#x27; = it&#x27;s optional). This distinction is frequently tested.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Parts of Speech</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Parts of Speech?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Parts of Speech are the categories into which words are classified based on their function in a sentence. English has eight traditional parts of speech: noun , pronoun , adjective , verb , adverb , preposition , conjunction , and interjection . A word may belong to different parts of speech depending on how it is used in a particular sentence. For example, &#x27;run&#x27; can be a verb (&#x27;I run every day&#x27;) or a noun (&#x27;That was a good run&#x27;).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Noun: Definition and Types</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A noun is a word that names a person, place, thing, idea, or quality. Nouns are classified into several types: Proper Nouns name specific individuals or places (e.g., Kathmandu , Sita , Nepal ) and are always capitalised. Common Nouns name general items (e.g., city , girl , country ). Collective Nouns refer to a group considered as a whole (e.g., team , herd , committee ). Material Nouns name substances (e.g., gold , water , wood ). Abstract Nouns name ideas or qualities (e.g., honesty , bravery , freedom ). Countable Nouns can be counted and have plural forms; Uncountable Nouns cannot be counted and take singular verbs.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Pronoun: Types and Uses</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A pronoun is a word that takes the place of a noun to avoid repetition. Personal Pronouns refer to specific people or things: I, you, he, she, it, we, they (and their objective forms: me, him, her, us, them). Reflective Pronouns end in -self/-selves and are used when the subject and object are the same (e.g., &#x27;She taught herself&#x27;). Relative Pronouns (who, whom, whose, which, that) connect clauses to nouns. Demonstrative Pronouns (this, that, these, those) point to specific things. Indefinite Pronouns (some, any, none, all, each, either, neither) refer to non-specific entities. Interrogative Pronouns (who, whom, which, what) are used to ask questions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Verb: Action and State</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>A verb is the most important part of speech — it expresses action or state of being. Action Verbs show physical or mental activity (e.g., run , think , write ). State Verbs (also called stative verbs) describe conditions rather than actions (e.g., be , seem , belong , know ). Transitive Verbs require a direct object (e.g., &#x27;She kicked the ball&#x27;). Intransitive Verbs do not take an object (e.g., &#x27;He slept &#x27;). Linking Verbs (be, seem, become, appear) connect the subject to a complement. Auxiliary (Helping) Verbs (be, do, have) combine with main verbs to form tenses, voices, and moods.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Adjective: Describing Nouns</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>An adjective modifies or describes a noun or pronoun, providing more information about its quality, quantity, or identity. Descriptive Adjectives describe qualities (e.g., beautiful , tall , intelligent ). Demonstrative Adjectives (this, that, these, those) identify specific nouns. Quantitative Adjectives (some, many, few, little, enough) indicate quantity. Numeral Adjectives (one, first, double) indicate number or order. Possessive Adjectives (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) show ownership. Interrogative Adjectives (which, what, whose) modify nouns in questions. Adjectives generally precede the noun they modify (&#x27;a red car&#x27;), but some follow linking verbs (&#x27;The sky is blue &#x27;).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Adverb: Modifying More Than Verbs</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>An adverb modifies verbs, adjectives, other adverbs, or even entire sentences. It answers questions such as how? , when? , where? , why? , to what extent? Adverbs of Manner describe how something happens (e.g., quickly , carefully , well ). Adverbs of Time (now, yesterday, soon) indicate when. Adverbs of Place (here, there, everywhere) indicate where. Adverbs of Frequency (always, often, never) indicate how often. Adverbs of Degree (very, too, almost, quite) indicate intensity. Many adverbs are formed by adding -ly to adjectives, but important exceptions exist: fast (not &#x27;fastly&#x27;), hard vs. hardly (different meanings), late vs. lately .</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Prepositions</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is a Preposition?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A preposition is a word (or group of words) that shows the relationship between a noun (or pronoun) and another word in the sentence. Prepositions typically indicate time (at, on, in, before, after), place (at, in, on, under, above, between), and direction (to, into, towards, through, across). Prepositions are among the most frequently tested elements in NEB English exams, appearing in gap-filling, error correction, and transformation exercises.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Prepositions of Time</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>At is used for precise times: at 5 o&#x27;clock, at noon, at night, at midnight, at sunrise. On is used for days and dates: on Monday, on 15th August, on my birthday, on Christmas Day. In is used for months, years, seasons, centuries, and longer periods: in January, in 2024, in summer, in the 21st century. Exceptions: &#x27;at night&#x27; (not &#x27;in night&#x27;), &#x27;at weekends&#x27; (British English), &#x27;on weekends&#x27; (American English). &#x27;During&#x27; is used with a period: during the war, during the movie. &#x27;For&#x27; indicates duration: for two hours, for three days. &#x27;Since&#x27; indicates a starting point: since Monday, since 1990.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Prepositions of Place</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>At is used for specific points or locations: at the door, at the station, at home, at the bottom. In is used for enclosed spaces, larger areas, and containers: in the room, in Kathmandu, in the box, in the world. On is used for surfaces and contact: on the table, on the wall, on the floor, on the page. Under indicates something below and covered: under the table, under water. Below indicates lower position but not necessarily covered: temperature below zero. Above indicates higher position: temperature above freezing. Over indicates movement across or covering: fly over the city, put a blanket over the baby.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Prepositions of Direction and Movement</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>To indicates movement toward a destination: go to school, send to Kathmandu. Into indicates movement from outside to inside: jump into the pool, pour water into the glass. Towards indicates direction without necessarily reaching the destination: walk towards the market. Through indicates movement from one end to the other inside something: walk through the tunnel, drive through the forest. Across indicates movement from one side to the other on a surface: swim across the river, walk across the street. Along indicates movement following a line: walk along the riverbank, drive along the highway.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Common Prepositional Phrases (Fixed Collocations)</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Many prepositions are used in fixed expressions that must be memorised. Common examples tested in NEB exams: afraid of , angry with (person) / angry about (thing), ask for , believe in , depend on , discover (no preposition — direct object), differ from , excited about , good at (skill), good for (beneficial), interested in , join in , laugh at , listen to , look at , look for (search) vs. look after (take care of), married to (not &#x27;with&#x27;), pay for , popular with , suffer from , think about / think of / think about (different nuances), worry about , wait for , write to (a person) / write in (a language).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Prepositions After Adjectives</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Certain adjectives are consistently followed by specific prepositions. Accustomed to , addicted to , aware of , capable of , fond of , guilty of , ignorant of , proud of , ready for , responsible for , short of , suspicious of , similar to , sorry for , thankful for , victorious over . These collocations are tested extensively in NEB gap-filling exercises. Memorising these pairings is essential for accuracy. Note that &#x27;different from&#x27; (standard) vs. &#x27;different to&#x27; (British informal) vs. &#x27;different than&#x27; (American informal).</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Subject-Verb Agreement</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Subject-Verb Agreement?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Subject-verb agreement is the grammatical rule that the verb must match its subject in number (singular or plural) and person (first, second, third). A singular subject takes a singular verb; a plural subject takes a plural verb. This is one of the most frequently tested grammar rules in NEB English exams, appearing in gap-filling, error correction, and multiple-choice questions.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Basic Rule: Singular vs. Plural</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The fundamental rule: Singular subjects take singular verbs (usually ending in -s in present tense). Plural subjects take plural verbs (no -s in present tense). Examples: &#x27;The boy runs &#x27; (singular subject + singular verb). &#x27;The boys run &#x27; (plural subject + plural verb). &#x27;She is here&#x27; vs. &#x27;They are here&#x27;. Note that the verb &#x27;to be&#x27; is irregular: I am, you are, he/she/it is, we are, they are. This irregularity is a common source of errors.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Subjects Joined by &#x27;And&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>When two or more subjects are joined by &#x27;and&#x27; , they usually take a plural verb: &#x27;Rama and Shyam are friends.&#x27; &#x27;The teacher and the principal are coming.&#x27; Exception: When the two nouns joined by &#x27;and&#x27; refer to the same person or thing, use a singular verb: &#x27;The singer and songwriter is performing tonight&#x27; (one person who is both singer and songwriter). &#x27;Bread and butter is my favourite snack&#x27; (a single combination).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Subjects Joined by &#x27;Or&#x27; / &#x27;Nor&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>When subjects are joined by &#x27;or&#x27; , &#x27;nor&#x27; , &#x27;either...or&#x27; , or &#x27;neither...nor&#x27; , the verb agrees with the nearer subject (proximity rule). Examples: &#x27;Neither the teacher nor the students were present.&#x27; (nearer subject &#x27;students&#x27; is plural → plural verb). &#x27;Either the students or the teacher is responsible.&#x27; (nearer subject &#x27;teacher&#x27; is singular → singular verb). &#x27;Neither Ram nor his friends are coming.&#x27; This proximity rule is consistently tested in NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Indefinite Pronouns as Subjects</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Most indefinite pronouns take singular verbs: each, every, either, neither, one, someone, somebody, something, anyone, anybody, anything, no one, nobody, nothing, everyone, everybody, everything . Examples: &#x27;Each of the boys has a pen.&#x27; &#x27;Nobody knows the answer.&#x27; &#x27;Everyone is present.&#x27; However, &#x27;both, few, several, many&#x27; take plural verbs: &#x27;Both of them are coming.&#x27; &#x27;Few of the students have passed.&#x27; &#x27;Several of the books are missing.&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>&#x27;Each&#x27; and &#x27;Every&#x27;: Singular Verbs</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>The words &#x27;each&#x27; and &#x27;every&#x27; always take singular verbs, even when followed by a plural noun in a prepositional phrase. &#x27;Each of the students has a book.&#x27; (NOT &#x27;have&#x27;). &#x27;Every boy and girl has a seat.&#x27; (NOT &#x27;have&#x27;). &#x27;Each&#x27; emphasises individuality within a group; &#x27;every&#x27; emphasises the group as a whole. Both require singular verbs because they refer to individuals one by one.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Tenses</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Tenses?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Tense is a verb form that shows the time of an action or state — past, present, or future. English has three main tenses: Present , Past , and Future . Each main tense has four aspects: Simple , Continuous (Progressive), Perfect , and Perfect Continuous . This gives English a total of twelve tense forms. Tense is one of the most important grammar topics in the NEB Class 11 English syllabus and appears frequently in both objective and descriptive questions.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Present Simple Tense</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The present simple tense is used for: (1) habits and routines — &#x27;I wake up at six every day.&#x27; (2) General truths and facts — &#x27;Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius.&#x27; (3) Permanent situations — &#x27;She lives in Pokhara.&#x27; (4) Scheduled events — &#x27;The train leaves at 9 AM.&#x27; The form is: subject + base verb (add -s/-es for third person singular). Negative: do not/does not + base verb. Question: Do/Does + subject + base verb? Time markers: always, usually, often, sometimes, never, every day/week/year.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Present Continuous Tense</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>The present continuous tense describes actions happening right now or around the present time. Form: subject + am/is/are + verb-ing. Uses: (1) Actions happening at the moment of speaking — &#x27;She is writing a letter.&#x27; (2) Temporary situations — &#x27;I am staying with my cousin this week.&#x27; (3) Future arrangements — &#x27;We are meeting them tomorrow.&#x27; (4) Changing situations — &#x27;The climate is getting warmer.&#x27; Important: Stative verbs (know, believe, belong, love, hate, seem, understand) are generally NOT used in continuous forms. We say &#x27;I know him&#x27;, not &#x27;I am knowing him&#x27;.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Present Perfect Tense</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>The present perfect tense connects the past with the present. Form: subject + has/have + past participle. Uses: (1) Actions completed at an unspecified time — &#x27;I have visited Chitwan.&#x27; (2) Actions that started in the past and continue to the present — &#x27;She has lived here for ten years.&#x27; (3) Recent actions with present results — &#x27;I have lost my keys&#x27; (so I can&#x27;t enter now). (4) Life experiences — &#x27;He has read all of Shakespeare.&#x27; Time markers: already, yet, just, ever, never, recently, so far, up to now, since, for.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Present Perfect Continuous Tense</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The present perfect continuous emphasises the duration of an action that started in the past and continues to the present, or has just finished with visible results. Form: subject + has/have + been + verb-ing. Example: &#x27;I have been studying English since 2019.&#x27; Compare with present perfect: &#x27;I have studied English for five years&#x27; (focus on completion/amount) vs. &#x27;I have been studying English for five years&#x27; (focus on the ongoing activity). Both are grammatically correct but carry slightly different emphasis.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Past Simple Tense</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>The past simple tense describes completed actions at a specific time in the past. Form: subject + past form of verb (regular verbs add -ed; irregular verbs have unique past forms). Uses: (1) Completed actions — &#x27;I went to Kathmandu last year.&#x27; (2) Series of completed actions — &#x27;He woke up, had breakfast, and left for school.&#x27; (3) Past habits — &#x27;I played cricket every evening when I was young.&#x27; Time markers: yesterday, last week/year, ago, in 2010, when I was a child. Regular past form: add -ed (walked, played). Irregular past forms must be memorised (go→went, see→saw, eat→ate).</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Active and Passive Voice</td><td>Active and Passive Voice covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Conjunctions</td><td>Conjunctions covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Determiners</td><td>Determiners covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Modal Verbs</td><td>Modal Verbs covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Parts of Speech</td><td>Parts of Speech covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Prepositions</td><td>Prepositions covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Subject-Verb Agreement</td><td>Subject-Verb Agreement covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Tenses</td><td>Tenses covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table></body></html>